[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"75","stub":{"id":"75","section":"CR 409 to Guntersville Lake","altname":"Lower Short","river":"Short Creek","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-ALB"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"The most common put-in is on Martling Road (CR 409), with the other option being the stream crossing at AL 75. It is tempting to put in on Hustleville Road directly above the falls, but the county has put up \"No Parking\" signs at the request of the landowners.\n\nThere is a large gravel pulloff on Martling Road on the downstream, river left side of the bridge. Park here and carry your boat down to the river under the bridge.","id":"106979","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Put-In (Martling Road)","distance":"0","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"waterfall","description":"The falls are very friendly and can be run just about anywhere that has water going over. Depending on where you go it’s an 18 or 20 foot drop. The traditional line is about 10 feet off the left bank, over the top of a small curler. Just don’t go too far left, because if you land on the wrong side of the boil at the bottom, it will shove you into the mildly undercut shelf on the bottom left, where an amusing display of carping will likely ensue. Set safety from on top of this shelf. Eddy out behind it, hike back up and run it again. You can paddle behind the falls on the river right side, and watch your friends go over from behind the curtain. Pretty neat.","id":"106981","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Short Creek Falls","distance":"3.1","difficulty":"III+","approximate":false,"photoID":"oG3d8dQ7yGLfY4sopJ34i"},{"type":"waterfall","description":"After you get bored of the falls, a few class II-III boogie rapids follow. Keep an eye on the scenery ahead; when you see a sheer cliff face ahead on the right as the river bends left, Grotto Falls is coming up. Following a small slide, beach yourself on the large rock outcropping that divides the flow to scout Grotto. The main line is just a couple feet to the left of the rock you landed on, an easy 8-footer with big boof potential. At low water, keep your boat angled right to miss a piton rock on the left side of this drop. This can also be run further river left as a double drop, just stay away from the undercut bank.","id":"106982","location":{"latitude":"34.325648","longitude":"-86.194187"},"name":"Grotto Falls","distance":"3.8","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"BkrbVOARRHwhsrDi6d59c"},{"type":"hazard","description":"A few easy rapids follow Rock Cruncher. The next significant rapid starts after a long pool, where the river doglegs right and the left and center are obscured by large boulders. The creek widens significantly here and you'll bump and scrape through the top part of this rapid, which has a few possible routes; the most entertaining option is a tight 3' boof against the far left bank. When the creek begins to channelize again, be wary as the majority of the current barrels down into Little Woodall, a dangerously sticky hole which is known to recirculate boats and swimmers. The safe line is to power into the eddy to the left of the hole, beside an enormous rectangular rock, then wheelchair down the rocks which form the pool on the downstream side. The more adventurous may wish to run the hole off the left shoulder, but be prepared to set safety if anyone plans on doing so.","id":"106986","location":{"latitude":"34.332429","longitude":"-86.187554"},"name":"Little Woodall","distance":"4.5","photoID":"VPJmMbkle9ak2wOzbh6OS","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"After a number of wide, easy rapids, you'll reach the lake. If you work far right on the last rapid, you'll find a fun 4 foot boof to end the day with. Additionally, the eddy line on far river left (or is it lake left?) presents one last opportunity for stern squirts as the creek dumps into the lake.\n\nOnce you reach the flatwater, celebrate the fact that you made it down the notorious Short Crick, and prepare yourself for the 45-minute, 3-mile paddle out. Just follow the channel all the way to the boat ramp. When the lake doglegs to the left for the first time, don’t get too close to the island or you’ll run aground.","id":"106988","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Lake Guntersville","distance":"5.5","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"5299","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5657","description":"Kevin Thomas runs Confluence Rapid. At higher levels its advisable to sneak the hole on the left.","caption":"(RM) Confluence Rapid (aka Little Woodall)","poi_name":"Confluence Rapid (aka Little Woodall)","subject":"Kevin Thomas","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5299","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5299"}},{"id":"5296","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5657","description":"Peter Horne runs Tornado Sluice","caption":"(RM) Tornado Sluice","poi_name":"Tornado Sluice","subject":"Peter Horne","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":"106983","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5296","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5296"}},{"id":"17860","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9442","description":"short on right, scharam on left","caption":"confluence of short and scharam creeks","poi_name":"","subject":"confluence","author":"msmith","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/17860","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/17860"}},{"id":"5297","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5657","description":"Marshall Fox runs Divided Highway","caption":"(RM) Divided Highway","poi_name":"Divided Highway","subject":"Marshall Fox","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":"106984","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5297","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5297"}},{"id":"5300","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5657","description":"This is the most overlooked rapid on the run. It is also the last rapid before the 2 mile lake paddle. It can be found on the river right side of the island before the lake. There is a nice channel to get to the drop about half way down the islands, which you can see on the right side of the photo.","caption":"(RM) Lake Ledge","poi_name":"Lake Ledge","subject":"Peter Horne","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":"106986","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5300","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5300"}},{"id":"VPJmMbkle9ak2wOzbh6OS","ext":"jpg","post_id":"HZBA1lEA2r5nZgQMjiNOA","description":"Pike taking the sneak line on Little Woodall. The bad hole is visible on the left side of the photo, against the river right bank.","caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Kellis Kincaid","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2022-01-03T04:48:44.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/VPJmMbkle9ak2wOzbh6OS","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/VPJmMbkle9ak2wOzbh6OS"}},{"id":"13791","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8958","description":"","caption":"Backwards in the long boat","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Rob Beckman","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/13791","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/13791"}},{"id":"13790","ext":"mov","post_id":"8960","description":"","caption":"Kieth Yell on the NAWF RAMP","poi_name":"","subject":"Kieth Yell","author":"Rob Beckman","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at"